296:\section{Seed pools and injection mechanism}\label{sec:seeding}
297:
298:\subsection{Generators and pools}
299:For every instance we use pools of 1{,}024 i.i.d.\ solutions per generator,
300:each stored as a job-permutation plus its makespan interval under a
301:semi-active decoder. Four generators span a quality ladder:
302:\begin{itemize}
303:\item \textbf{MOR-$\varepsilon$}: most-operations-remaining dispatching
304:  with $\varepsilon=0.1$ random tie-perturbation (weak baseline);
305:\item \textbf{GT-$\varepsilon$}: randomised Giffler--Thompson with
306:  $\varepsilon$-noise inside the conflict set (active schedules; strong
307:  baseline);
308:\item \textbf{GP-$\varepsilon$}: a genetic-programming priority rule,
309:  evolved and irace-tuned offline, with GRASP-style $\varepsilon$-noise
310:  (strongest heuristic baseline);
311:\item \textbf{v2}: a deep-RL dispatching policy (deep-sets architecture,
312:  PPO, stochastic sampling of the policy for diversity), trained only on
313:  Taillard instances TA11--TA14 and developed on TA15--TA20. Its pool is
314:  the one structural exception to i.i.d.\ sampling: it is three
315:  consecutive blocks of $342/341/341$ solutions, one per training
316:  checkpoint, and the blocks differ measurably in quality (Kruskal--Wallis
317:  $p<10^{-6}$ on every instance tested). The consequence for the arms that
318:  use it is quantified in Section~\ref{sec:limits}. The policy also ranks
319:  candidate schedules by LEX2, whereas three of the four solvers rank by
320:  the midpoint, so these seeds are optimised under a different criterion
321:  from the one that later evaluates them.
322:\end{itemize}
323:Across the 71-instance pool collection the ladder is clean: mean relative
324:errors of 51.1\% (MOR) $>$ 30.3\% (GT) $>$ 21.4\% (GP) $\approx$ 21.2\%
325:(v2), with v2 exhibiting the highest structural diversity of the four
326:(0.95 vs.\ 0.92 for GP on a normalised disjunctive-order distance) and GP
327:slightly stronger on the largest size classes. A fifth, mixed pool
328:(equal parts v2/GT/GP; MOR is deliberately excluded, being the weakest
329:generator) is used by the \emph{mix} arm. All five pools --- the four generators and the mixture --- contain 1{,}024
330:solutions per instance, so no arm is sampled more richly than another.
331:
332:The mixed pool interleaves the three generators one entry at a time, so
333:that any contiguous window of 250 entries contains $84/83/83$ seeds of
334:v2/GT/GP to within one seed, whatever its offset.
335:
336:\subsection{Evaluation convention of the pools}\label{sec:audit}
337:Each pool line stores a permutation together with its interval makespan,
338:evaluated component-wise so that stored and solver-side objective values
339:coincide. The distinction matters because both interval conventions of
340:Section~\ref{sec:problem} occur in this literature and they do not agree:
341:against LEX2 aggregation the upper bound is identical on all
342:1{,}024 lines of every pool, while the lower bound differs on 1--22\% of
343:lines, by up to 98 time units on the $50\times20$ instances. Anyone
344:exchanging solutions between components that rank intervals differently
345:should expect the same.
346:
347:\subsection{Injection mechanism}
348:Seeding is deliberately minimal. A seeded arm with fraction $k/250$
349:replaces the first $k$ individuals of the initial population by pool lines;
350:the remaining $250-k$ individuals are generated by the solver's standard
351:random initialisation. For paired run $r\in\{0,\dots,29\}$ the arm takes
352:pool lines $[(r\cdot k)\bmod 1024,\,(r\cdot k+k)\bmod 1024)$ --- consecutive
353:blocks, no reordering or filtering, so each run draws from the generator's
354:true output distribution rather than from a curated subset. The 30 blocks
355:start at 30 distinct offsets and consecutive ones are disjoint, but with
356:$k=250$ and 1{,}024 pool lines the wraparound makes any two of them share
357:$22\%$ of their entries on average, and each pool line is drawn by $7.3$
358:of the 30 runs; the seeded populations are therefore not fully independent
359:across runs, which is one reason the unit of analysis throughout is the
360:instance rather than the run. Scout-bee replacements
361:during the run remain fully random. Seeds enter as raw permutations and are
362:re-decoded and re-evaluated by the receiving solver, so no foreign
363:objective values ever enter the search.
364:
365:\subsection{The cost of producing the seeds}\label{sec:gencost}
366:
367:Seeding is not free, and at the budgets where it helps most the cost is not
368:negligible. Generation was timed for the two heuristic generators on a
369:single thread: a pool of 1{,}024 solutions takes $91$\,s on $15\times15$
370:and $1{,}255$\,s on $50\times20$ for GP-$\varepsilon$, and $89$\,s to
371:$1{,}500$\,s for GT-$\varepsilon$, scaling roughly linearly in the number of
372:operations. The learned policy is the expensive one: measured per-rollout
373:inference of $0.60$\,s on $15\times15$ and $3.98$\,s on $50\times20$ puts it
374:six to ten times above the rules per solution. We did not time the v2 and
375:MOR-$\varepsilon$ pools directly and do not report an equivalent figure for
376:them.
377:
378:Two readings follow, and they differ by an order of magnitude. Producing the
379:250 seeds of a \emph{single} run costs from $22$\,s on $15\times15$ to
380:$306$\,s on $50\times20$ with the cheapest generator --- between four and
381:fifty-one times the tenth of the budget at which the anytime advantage is
382:largest, depending on solver and class. Amortised instead over the thirty
383:runs that share a pool, as in this study and in any repeated-solve setting,
384:the same cost falls to between $0.1$ and $7$ times that slice, and below it
385:in most cells. The short-budget result therefore holds as stated for a
386:practitioner who reuses a pool, and does not hold for one who generates
387:seeds to solve a single instance once.
388:
389:%% =========================================================================
390:\section{Pilot study: three ways to fool yourself}\label{sec:pilot}
391:
392:We first ran a full pilot on three clean instances
393:(tai15\_15\_05, tai20\_20\_02, tai30\_20\_04; lower bounds 1224, 1581 and 1952) with six solvers (the portfolio plus two isolation variants), seven
394:arms and 30 paired runs per cell. The pilot's role in this paper is
395:methodological: each of its three phases exposed an artefact that the final
396:design of Section~\ref{sec:design} removes.
397:
398:\subsection{Artefact 1: stagnation-triggered stopping}
399:Under the customary rule of 25 consecutive non-improving generations ---
400:the stopping criterion of every published solver in the portfolio ---
401:seeded GA arms terminated at the 25-generation floor (the solver could not
402:improve the injected solutions), while the unseeded control ran for
403:100--150 generations. The resulting ``final'' comparison charges the seeds
404:with the solver's inability to improve them. Any seeding study that uses
405:stagnation-based stopping inherits this bias.
406:
407:\subsection{Artefact 2: budget-dependent gains that reverse}
408:Re-running everything under a fixed budget of 200 generations (no
409:stagnation rule) appeared to settle matters: seeded GA arms beat the
410:control on the hardest instance by 34 units of expected makespan. A
411:convergence check, however, showed the GA control still improving at
412:budget exhaustion. At $5\times$ the budget (1{,}000 generations) the
413:control kept descending, overtook the stagnant seeded populations, and the
414:effect reversed sign (from $-34$ to $+67$ on tai30\_20\_04 with full v2
415:seeding). The same check showed the elitist ABC's seeded advantage
416:\emph{persisting} at convergence ($-46$ to $-35$ with GP seeds), and the
417:strong local-search solvers saturating at equal quality. A separate
418:isolation experiment --- attaching the identical tabu local search of
419:TS-N2 to a GA frame --- initially suggested the GA frame benefited from
420:seeds where the ABC frame did not; extending its time cap dissolved that
421:difference too, tracing it to budget starvation rather than to the frame.
422:\emph{Conclusion: no seeding claim about final quality is meaningful
423:without a convergence check of the unseeded control.}
424:
425:\subsection{Artefact 3: what ``faster'' means}
426:We measure convergence speed with two deliberately different metrics:
427:(A)~time to reach within 1\% of the \emph{arm's own} final value, and
428:(B)~time to reach the \emph{unseeded control's} final quality (tolerance
429:$+0.5\%$). Metric~A is self-referential: an arm that settles quickly on a
430:worse plateau scores well, so an apparent acceleration may be nothing more
431:than earlier stagnation. Metric~B removes that degree of freedom by fixing
432:a common target, at the cost of being undefined on instances where the arm
433:never reaches it --- which makes the proportion of instances reaching the
434:target as much a part of the result as the time itself. Reporting both,
435:with that proportion, prevents the most common over-claim in anytime
436:comparisons.
437:
438:\subsection{What survives the artefacts}
439:With all three corrections in place, the pilot's surviving picture is:
440:(i)~seeding improves converged quality for the elitist ABC on all three
441:instances (Wilcoxon, paired); (ii)~for solvers with strong local search
442:the final quality saturates but metric-A acceleration remains; (iii)~for
443:the plain GA the apparent gain is an artefact; (iv)~GP and v2 seeds are
444:statistically tied at the top of the generator ladder, MOR seeds never
445:help; and (v)~initial-population quality ranks exactly as the pool ladder
446:predicts. These five statements were fixed before the final experiment and
447:are what it tests at scale; Section~\ref{sec:res-hyp} reports which of them
448:survived.
449:
450:%% =========================================================================
451:\section{Final experimental design}\label{sec:design}
452:
453:\subsection{Instances}
454:All 60 interval Taillard instances TA1--TA70 excluding TA11--TA20, plus
455:ft10 (61 total). TA11--TA14 are the RL training set and TA15--TA20 the
456:RL/GP development set; excluding them is mandatory because they contaminate
457:the \emph{seed generators}. Twenty instances used for irace tuning of
458:TS-N2's hyper-parameters are retained but flagged: solver-tuning
459:contamination applies equally to every arm of that solver and cancels in
460:within-solver paired comparisons; a sensitivity analysis replicates all
461:conclusions with the flagged instances removed. Size classes range from
462:$15{\times}15$ to $50{\times}20$ (225--1{,}000 operations). Taillard's
463:largest class, TA71--TA80 ($100{\times}20$, 2{,}000 operations), is also
464:excluded: at twice the operation count of our largest class it falls
465:outside the budget calibration of Section~\ref{sec:design}, and extending
466:the design to it would require a separate calibration that we did not
467:perform. Consequently no seed pools were generated for either excluded
468:group.
469:
470:\subsection{Arms}
471:Seven arms per solver: the unseeded control A0; the generator ladder
472:MOR, GT, GP, v2 and mix at the pre-specified fraction $p^{*}$; and a
473:v2@50\% contrast arm. The fraction sub-study (Phase~0, pre-specified
474:decision rule combining converged quality in ABCE3 and time-to-target in
475:TS-N2 over eight stratified instances) selected $p^{*}=100\%$: the benefit
476:of v2 seeding is monotone in the fraction on both criteria, with no
477:interior optimum.
478:%% TODO: FTOP sub-study outcome (elitist selection of the best 250 pool
479:%% lines vs iid sampling) — decides whether the ladder seeds iid or elite.
480:
481:\subsection{Budgets and stopping}
482:Budgets are set per \emph{(solver, size class)} rather than per instance.
483:For each class we take one or two calibration instances, run the unseeded
484:control on them, and record $t_{\mathrm{conv}}$: the first CPU time at
485:which the run-averaged trace reaches within $0.1\%$ of its own final
486:value. The budget applied to every cell of that class is
487:\begin{equation}\label{eq:budget}
488:B(\text{solver},\text{class})=
489:\max\bigl\{60\,\mathrm{s},\;
490:\min\{1.5\,\tilde{t}_{\mathrm{conv}},\,900\,\mathrm{s}\}\bigr\},
491:\end{equation}
492:where $\tilde{t}_{\mathrm{conv}}$ is the median over that class's
493:calibration instances. The floor of 60\,s prevents degenerate budgets on
494:the small classes, where several solvers plateau in a few seconds. Every
495:arm of a cell receives the identical budget, which is what the
496:within-solver comparisons require. The eight calibration instances ---
497:\texttt{tai15\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai20\_20\_02}, \texttt{tai30\_15\_01},
498:\texttt{tai30\_20\_04}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_05},
499:\texttt{tai50\_20\_01} and \texttt{tai50\_20\_05} --- are stratified across
500:the size classes and are themselves members of the 61-instance evaluation
501:set; the control traces behind $t_{\mathrm{conv}}$ comprise five runs for
502:the GA and fEABC-LS and thirty for ABCE3 and TS-N2. The horizon of a class
503:is therefore the control's own convergence timescale, measured on one or
504:two of the instances to which it is later applied. What the endpoint
505:measures is thus performance at a control-calibrated horizon rather than a
506:solver-neutral approximation to convergence, a distinction we return to in
507:Section~\ref{sec:limits}.
508:
509:The cap of 900\,s is a feasibility constraint rather than a methodological
510:choice. The design comprises 58{,}560 runs; at the budgets of
511:(\ref{eq:budget}) it consumed roughly 4{,}900 CPU-hours and fourteen days
512:of wall-clock time on fourteen concurrent workers. Raising the cap would
513:scale that figure proportionally on the classes that reach it, which are
514:also the slowest. We therefore accept the cap and treat its consequence
515:explicitly: on a substantial fraction of cells the unseeded control is
516:still improving when the budget expires, so those cells cannot support a
517:converged-quality claim. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} identifies them and
518:reports the final-quality results both over all instances and restricted
519:to the cells that do satisfy the plateau rule.
520:
521:\subsection{Statistics}
522:30 runs per cell. Run $r$ of every arm of a solver is launched with the
523:same RNG seed index, which blocks the design by (instance, run index).
524:This is \emph{not} a common-random-numbers coupling: a seeded arm does not
525:draw from the generator to build its first $k$ individuals, so the
526:generator state diverges between arms immediately after initialisation and
527:the runs follow unrelated random trajectories thereafter. The pairing is
528:therefore a labelling of blocks rather than a variance-reduction device.
529:Signed-rank tests on such pairs remain valid --- under the null the
530:per-instance differences are still symmetric about zero --- but they are
531:conservative rather than more powerful, and we make no claim of matched
532:trajectories. Within-solver comparisons use
533:paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm correction per family, plus
534:Vargha--Delaney $\hat{A}_{12}$ effect sizes \cite{Vargha2000,Derrac2011};
535:generator rankings use Friedman tests with post-hoc analysis. Anytime
536:results are reported as expected-makespan-versus-CPU-time curves and as the
537:two time-to-target metrics of Section~\ref{sec:pilot}, the latter
538:accompanied by the proportion of instances on which the target is reached
539:--- which carries the information a success-rate curve would convey at the
540:budget limit.
541:
542:%% =========================================================================
543:\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
544:
545:All results below come from the full factorial design of
546:Section~\ref{sec:design}: 61 instances $\times$ 7 arms $\times$ 4 solvers
547:$\times$ 30 RNG-paired runs (58{,}560 runs). Every instance--arm cell
548:contains exactly 30 runs; no cell was imputed or dropped.
549:
550:\subsection{Reference for relative deviations}\label{sec:rpd-ref}
551:
552:Relative percentage deviation (RPD) is measured against published lower
553:bounds rather than against best-known values produced by our own group,
554:which would bias RPD downwards. Our interval instances are obtained from
555:Taillard's benchmark \cite{taillard1993benchmarks} by a symmetric
556:perturbation of each duration; we verified across all 57{,}450 operations
557:that $\mathrm{mid}(p_{ij})=c_{ij}$ exactly and that routings are unchanged.
558:Because the makespan is composed only of additions and maxima, and because
559:$\mathrm{mid}(A{+}B)=\mathrm{mid}(A)+\mathrm{mid}(B)$ while
560:$\mathrm{mid}(\max(A,B))\ge\max(\mathrm{mid}(A),\mathrm{mid}(B))$ under the
561:componentwise maximum, induction over the schedule DAG yields
562:$\mathrm{mid}(C_{\max}^{\mathrm{int}}(x))\ge C_{\max}^{\mathrm{crisp}}(x)$
563:for \emph{every} schedule $x$, and therefore
564:\begin{equation}\label{eq:lb}
565:\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]^{*}\;\ge\;C_{\max}^{\mathrm{crisp}*}\;\ge\;\mathrm{LB},
566:\end{equation}
567:so the crisp lower bound is a valid (if not tight) bound for the interval
568:problem. We take LB from the consolidated table of
569:Coupvent des Graviers et al.\ \cite{coupvent2025bounds}; the instance
570:mapping was checked rather than assumed, with 19 of 60 instances showing
571:exact agreement between the published bound and the machine-load bound
572:recomputed from the instance files. For the remaining 41 the check is an
573:inequality, which is necessary but does not identify an instance, and we
574:do not claim more than that.
575:
576:An exact identification is nevertheless available to any reader, and we
577:supply what it needs. Because each interval was built as
578:$[p_o-\delta,\,p_o+\delta]$, it is symmetric about the original crisp
579:duration, so the midpoint recovers that duration exactly; across the 61
580:instances every midpoint is integral, with no exception. We therefore
581:release the recovered crisp matrices, one per instance with a hash, so that
582:identification reduces to an element-wise comparison against Taillard's
583:published files. The recovery procedure itself is validated on the one
584:instance of the set whose crisp form is unambiguous: \texttt{ft10} recovers
585:to the Fisher--Thompson $10\times10$ matrix exactly.
586:
587:Inequality~(\ref{eq:lb}) also yields a falsifiable prediction: no run may
588:ever fall below its bound. Across all 58{,}560 runs there is not a single
589:violation. This is a consistency check rather than a proof of correctness:
590:many errors would preserve the inequality, and it is the released crisp
591:matrices, not this check, that would settle the mapping. Moreover the bound
592:is
593:\emph{attained exactly} on six instances --- \texttt{tai30\_15\_05},
594:\texttt{tai50\_15\_01}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_02}, \texttt{tai50\_15\_04},
595:\texttt{tai50\_15\_07} and \texttt{tai50\_20\_09} --- where our portfolio
596:reaches $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ equal to the proven crisp optimum. By
597:(\ref{eq:lb}) these schedules are therefore optimal for the interval
598:instance as well, which both certifies optimality on those six instances
599:and confirms that the inequality is tight rather than vacuous.
600:
601:\subsection{Which cells converged}\label{sec:res-conv}
602:
603:The plateau rule of Section~\ref{sec:design} --- improvement in
604:$\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ below $0.1\%$ over the final tenth of the budget ---
605:is applied to each control \emph{run} against the solution the solver
606:returns, rather than to the run-averaged curve, since averaging thirty
607:traces can hide a subset that is still improving; a cell counts as
608:converged when at least $90\%$ of its runs satisfy it. This classifies 111
609:of the 244 control cells as \emph{anytime-only}: 49 of 61 for the GA, 25
610:for fEABCLS, 22 for TSN2 and 15 for ABCE3. The GA fails on every instance
611:of the four largest classes, and the $50\times20$ class defeats every
612:solver but ABCE3, whose own failures are otherwise confined to
613:$15\times15$, where a third of its runs are still improving when the
614:$60$\,s floor stops them. What the classification is and is not sensitive
615:to is reported in Section~\ref{sec:res-threats}; the per-cell
616:classifications are released with the data.
617:
