862:
863:Three of the four solvers need a word beyond the table.
864:
865:\paragraph{ABCE3 gains unambiguously.} MIX improves it by $1.14$ percentage
866:points of RPD, interval $[-1.36,-0.93]$, $p<10^{-3}$; V2, V2H and GP
867:likewise. Correcting over all 24 contrasts of the study rather than within
868:solvers changes nothing: the same 15 remain significant.
869:
870:\paragraph{The GA is harmed by homogeneous pools, not by seeding.} The
871:mixed pool's difference is $+0.055$ points, with an interval spanning zero
872:and $p=0.55$, while every \emph{single-generator} pool is significantly
873:harmful: MOR ($+2.18$), GT ($+0.95$), V2 ($+0.53$) and GP ($+0.47$). Read
874:with Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}, the picture is a solver damaged by
875:homogeneous seeds under an insufficient budget.
876:
877:\paragraph{The improvement on TSN2 does not reach significance.} This is
878:the reading that changes most. The signed-rank test over the 61
879:per-instance differences gives $p=0.065$ before correction and $p=0.26$
880:after, so MIX cannot be declared better than the control by a test that
881:weights every instance equally. The bootstrap interval for the mean
882:difference, $[-0.35,-0.045]$, nonetheless excludes zero. The two statistics
883:are not in conflict: the signed-rank test asks about the median difference,
884:which is essentially zero because 51 of the 61 instances are unaffected,
885:while the mean is pulled negative by the ten $50\times20$ instances that
886:carry $94.8\%$ of the effect (Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class}). We therefore
887:state the TSN2 result as what it is --- a small mean improvement
888:concentrated in one size class, with an interval that excludes zero but
889:without significance under an instance-weighted test --- and not as a
890:general improvement of the strongest solver. Establishing the latter would
891:require either more instances in the affected regime or a budget under
892:which more of them behave like $50\times20$.
893:
894:\subsection{The effect scales with instance size}\label{sec:res-size}
895:
896:Aggregate figures conceal a strong interaction with problem size.
897:Figure~\ref{fig:size} plots the relative change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$
898:under MIX seeding against instance class, ordered by number of operations
899:(100 to 1000).
900:
901:\begin{figure}[htbp]
902:\centering
903:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figs/main_effect_by_size.png}
904:\caption{Relative change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ under MIX seeding with
905:respect to the unseeded control, by instance class (ordered by number of
906:operations). Negative values favour seeding. The benefit for ABCE3 and
907:TSN2 grows with instance size, whereas the penalty for the GA persists.}
908:\label{fig:size}
909:\end{figure}
910:
911:Two trends emerge. For ABCE3 the gain deepens with size, from $-0.21\%$ on
912:the smallest instance to $-1.24\%$ on $20\times20$ and $-1.50\%$ on
913:$50\times20$. For TSN2 the effect is flat and near zero up to $50\times15$
914:and then drops sharply to $-1.06\%$ on $50\times20$ --- and on that class
915:\emph{every} seeded arm beats the control, including GP ($\Delta=-30.9$),
916:MOR ($\Delta=-25.6$) and GT ($\Delta=-19.6$), all three of which are
917:harmful everywhere else. The GA moves in the opposite direction, remaining
918:between $+0.05\%$ and $+0.48\%$ on the four largest classes.
919:
920:Table~\ref{tab:tsn2class} makes the concentration explicit for TSN2, and it
921:is the single most important caveat in this paper. The ten $50\times20$
922:instances account for $94.8\%$ of the aggregate improvement; the remaining
923:51 instances contribute almost nothing. Reporting only the pooled figure of
924:Table~\ref{tab:rpd} would therefore misrepresent the result as a general
925:improvement of the strongest solver, which it is not.
926:
927:\begin{table}[htbp]
928:\centering
929:\caption{Decomposition of the MIX seeding effect on TSN2 by instance class.
930:$\Delta$ is the mean signed change in $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ (negative
931:favours seeding); the last column is each class's share of the total
932:improvement.}
933:\label{tab:tsn2class}
934:\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
935:\toprule
936:Class & $n$ & $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ A0 & $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ MIX & $\Delta$ (\%) & Share \\
937:\midrule
938:$10\times10$ & 1  & 937.1  & 944.5  & $+0.78$ & --- \\
939:$15\times15$ & 10 & 1253.6 & 1254.3 & $+0.06$ & --- \\
940:$20\times20$ & 10 & 1668.8 & 1669.1 & $+0.01$ & --- \\
941:$30\times15$ & 10 & 1844.8 & 1843.6 & $-0.06$ & 3.6\% \\
942:$30\times20$ & 10 & 2052.6 & 2051.4 & $-0.06$ & 3.4\% \\
943:$50\times15$ & 10 & 2780.3 & 2779.2 & $-0.04$ & 3.2\% \\
944:$\mathbf{50\times20}$ & 10 & \textbf{2970.1} & \textbf{2938.4} & $\mathbf{-1.06}$ & \textbf{94.8\%} \\
945:\bottomrule
946:\end{tabular}
947:\end{table}
948:
949:The interpretation is a budget-starvation argument. On $50\times20$
950:instances (1000 operations) even a strong tabu search cannot,
951:within the 900\,s cap, recover from a random start the structure that a
952:constructive heuristic supplies for free. Seeding does not make the search
953:better; it removes a phase of the search that the budget cannot afford.
954:This predicts that the benefit of seeding should be reported as a function
955:of the budget-to-size ratio rather than as a single aggregate figure, and
956:that published seeding gains obtained on small instances will not transfer.
957:
958:\subsection{Anytime behaviour}\label{sec:res-anytime}
959:
960:Final quality alone cannot distinguish a solver that is genuinely improved
961:by seeding from one that is merely given a head start. Because per-solver
962:budgets range from 60\,s to 900\,s across size classes, absolute time is
963:not comparable across instances; we therefore normalise each trace to the
964:fraction of its own budget consumed, the standard convention for aggregated
965:anytime curves, and average over instances on a common grid so that no
966:instance enters or leaves mid-curve. Figure~\ref{fig:anytimeall} shows the
967:resulting profiles over all 61 instances, and Table~\ref{tab:budgetfrac}
968:gives the values at five budget fractions.
