1028:
1029:One caution applies to these curves. They record the best individual
1030:\emph{in the population} at each sampling point rather than the solution
1031:the solver finally returns, and the last sample precedes termination by up
1032:to one sampling interval. In practice the two agree closely: the curve
1033:endpoint differs from the final quality of Table~\ref{tab:rpd} by a median
1034:of $0.000\%$ on ABCE3, $0.003\%$ on fEABCLS and $0.029\%$ on the GA. On
1035:TSN2 the curve ends $0.041\%$ \emph{below} the returned quality, because
1036:TS-N2 ranks internally by LEX2 while the curve tracks
1037:$\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$, so a schedule that is better on the midpoint need
1038:not be the one it returns. We nonetheless confine every cross-arm
1039:comparison to a single solver, where all arms are measured identically.
1040:
1041:Within that scope, the aggregate view exposes a result the final-quality
1042:tables cannot show: \emph{at short budgets seeding is a large win for every
1043:solver in the portfolio, but by margins that differ by an order of
1044:magnitude between them}. At 10\% of budget MIX cuts mean RPD from
1045:$14.84\%$ to $8.91\%$ on fEABCLS and from $12.48\%$ to $7.89\%$ on TSN2,
1046:but only from $12.28\%$ to $10.23\%$ on ABCE3 and from $13.58\%$ to
1047:$12.10\%$ on the GA --- reductions of $40\%$, $37\%$, $17\%$ and $11\%$ in
1048:the excess over the lower bound, equivalent to $5.2\%$, $4.1\%$, $1.8\%$
1049:and $1.3\%$ of makespan. The two largest short-budget gains therefore
1050:accrue to the two \emph{strongest} solvers, which is the reverse of the
1051:ordering at the budget limit. Both are the solvers whose control spends its
1052:opening on an unseeded random population before local search can act:
1053:fEABCLS falls from $14.84\%$ to $5.60\%$ between a tenth and a quarter of
1054:its budget, and seeding buys precisely that opening. The practical reading
1055:is that the question ``does seeding help?'' is ill-posed unless the budget
1056:is specified, and that which solver benefits most depends on where in its
1057:own trajectory the budget cuts.
1058:
1059:The GA panel isolates the reversal. Its seeded arms lead until roughly
1060:20\% of the budget, after which the control crosses and stays below to the
1061:end. This is the anytime signature of the consolidation failure analysed in
1062:Section~\ref{sec:whyga}: the seeded population is ahead while quality is
1063:inherited from the seeds, and falls behind as soon as progress must come
1064:from recombination. ABCE3 shows the opposite profile, a gap that opens
1065:immediately and never closes.
1066:
1067:The generator ranking is also stable along the whole trajectory, not merely
1068:at the end. MOR is the uppermost seeded curve in all four panels and GT the
1069:next, so their disadvantage is not an artefact of where measurement stops:
1070:from a quarter of the budget onwards both are worse than the control on the
1071:two ABC variants. At a tenth of the budget they are still ahead of it,
1072:which says more about how poor an unseeded random population is at that
1073:point than about any merit of theirs. MIX is the lowest curve in three of
1074:four panels for essentially the entire run. Two observations qualify the
1075:ranking. First, GP is competitive with MIX at very short budgets --- on
1076:TSN2 at 10\% of budget GP reaches $7.94\%$ against MIX's $7.89\%$ --- so
1077:the advantage of the mixed pool \emph{widens as the search proceeds} rather
1078:than being inherited from higher initial seed quality. That is exactly what
1079:one expects if the operative variable is pool diversity rather than seed
1080:quality, and Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual} confirms it directly. Second,
1081:the seeded \emph{fraction} interacts with the solver, and not in one
1082:direction. On the GA, seeding half the population beats seeding all of it:
1083:the paired per-instance difference between V2H and V2 is $-0.39$ points of
1084:RPD, interval $[-0.54,-0.26]$, $p<10^{-3}$. On ABCE3 the comparison
1085:reverses with the same confidence, $+0.12$ points, $[+0.05,+0.18]$,
1086:$p<10^{-3}$, in favour of seeding the whole population; on fEABCLS and TSN2
1087:the two are indistinguishable ($p=0.58$ and $p=0.35$). Retaining random
1088:material is therefore not a general recommendation. It helps the solver
1089:that cannot consolidate a fully seeded population within its budget, which
1090:is the same solver, and on our reading the same mechanism, as the reversal
1091:of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}. Section~\ref{sec:res-fraction} shows that
1092:this lever is independent of pool composition and adds to it.
1093:
1094:Figure~\ref{fig:anytime} restricts the same analysis to the $50\times20$
1095:class in absolute time, where the seeding effect on the strong solvers is
1096:concentrated.
1097:
1098:\begin{figure}[htbp]
1099:\centering
1100:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figs/anytime_50x20.png}
1101:\caption{Convergence on the $50\times20$ class (10 instances, 30 runs).
1102:Mean RPD with respect to the published lower bound against CPU time. Time
1103:axes differ because budgets are calibrated per solver and size class.
1104:Markers show the instants at which the traces are actually sampled, drawn
1105:where that resolution is coarse enough to matter.}
1106:\label{fig:anytime}
1107:\end{figure}
1108:
1109:Two features are specific to this class. ABCE3's control flattens at
1110:approximately 11.2\% while both seeded arms flatten near 9.7\%, a gap
1111:present from the first seconds that never closes --- a genuine improvement
1112:in converged quality rather than an acceleration. Its panel is visibly
1113:coarser than the others because its budget on this class is the $60$\,s
1114:floor of Equation~\eqref{eq:budget} while the trace is sampled every
1115:$5$\,s: thirteen measurements per run, against 165 for fEABCLS. The steps
1116:are where the data are, not an artefact of the estimator. TSN2's control
1117:exhibits a
1118:sharp step near 50\,s, when the tabu phase first engages, and thereafter
1119:descends steadily; MIX remains below it over the entire trajectory and is
1120:still below at the budget limit (approximately 3.3\% against 4.1\%). The
1121:separation is therefore not an artefact of where the budget stops.
1122:
1123:One methodological consequence deserves emphasis. The GA result would have
1124:been reported as a \emph{success} under any budget shorter than its
1125:crossover point, which is precisely the artefact documented in
1126:Section~\ref{sec:pilot}. Since seeding studies commonly fix a budget
1127:without verifying convergence, this is a plausible source of optimistic
1128:seeding claims in the literature, and it argues for reporting anytime
1129:profiles rather than single-budget summaries.
1130:
1131:\subsection{Time to target}\label{sec:res-ttt}
1132:
1133:Anytime curves show \emph{that} seeded arms lead early; time-to-target
1134:quantifies \emph{by how much}. We report the two metrics of
1135:Section~\ref{sec:pilot}: (A) the time to come within 1\% of the arm's own
1136:final value, and (B) the time to reach the unseeded control's final quality
1137:within $+0.5\%$. Both are expressed as a fraction of the per-instance
1138:budget so that classes with different budgets can be pooled, and both are
1139:medians over the 61 instances. Metric B is reported together with the
1140:proportion of instances on which the arm reaches the target at all, since a
1141:median computed only over successes is meaningless without it.
