1184:
1185:Metric A behaves exactly as the pilot warned, and one figure shows why. On
1186:the GA the earliest plateau belongs to MOR ($0.49$ of the budget against the
1187:control's $0.60$), and MOR is the worst generator on all four solvers: an
1188:arm that settles quickly on a poor value scores well \emph{because} it is
1189:poor. We report the metric to document that it must not be used alone, and
1190:do not tabulate it further.
1191:
1192:Metric B needs care of a different kind. An arm that never reaches the
1193:target on an instance has no time-to-target there, and dropping such
1194:instances would summarise only the successes --- precisely discarding the
1195:cases where the arm does worst. The reach rate and the times must therefore
1196:enter the analysis together.
1197:
1198:Two properties of the design constrain how. First, the arms are paired:
1199:each seeded arm and its control share the instance, the budget and the
1200:target, so a comparison that treats them as independent groups is not
1201:available. Second, and more restrictive, the target on an instance is the
1202:control's own endpoint, so the control attains it on all 61 instances by
1203:construction. Its sample cannot be censored, whereas the seeded samples
1204:can. A log-rank test against such a reference contrasts a censored sample
1205:with one that is uncensorable by definition, and we therefore do not report
1206:one.
1207:
1208:What survives both constraints is a paired analysis of the recorded times,
1209:with a non-reacher recorded at the budget, $T=1.0$. The quantity the mean
1210:then estimates is the restricted mean time $E[\min(T,1)]$, for which that
1211:recording is a definition rather than an approximation. The primary
1212:contrast is the sign test on the paired earlier/later counts, ties
1213:excluded, taken against the null proportion the design itself produces
1214:rather than against $0.5$: because the target is drawn from the control's
1215:own endpoint, a split-half calibration under a true null
1216:(Section~\ref{sec:res-threats}) puts that proportion at $0.46$--$0.49$
1217:depending on the solver. The paired difference in restricted mean time
1218:($\Delta$RMTT) is reported alongside it with a bootstrap interval over
1219:instances. Both are Holm-corrected over the seven seeded arms of each solver.
1220:No test is reported for the control, which attains its own target on all 61
1221:instances by construction; ``reach'' is the percentage of instances on
1222:which an arm attains it at all.
1223:
1224:Under this treatment ABCE3 stands out. Its mixed pool reaches the control's
1225:\emph{final} quality on every instance and earlier on 57 of the 59 that are
1226:not ties, cutting restricted mean time from $0.38$ to $0.12$ of the budget;
1227:V2 and V2H do nearly as well. These are unambiguous ``same quality,
1228:sooner'' results, and they agree with the converged-quality finding for
1229:that solver. TSN2 joins it more modestly: its mixed pool is earlier on 42
1230:instances and later on 15 ($p=0.003$), and reaches the target on $93\%$ of
1231:them against the control's $100\%$ by construction.
1232:
1233:The other two solvers give the opposite answer. \emph{Five of the GA's six
1234:seeded arms are significantly slower} to the control's own final quality;
1235:only the mixed pool escapes, and narrowly (18 earlier, 37 later,
1236:$p=0.057$), though its restricted mean time is clearly worse ($+0.09$,
1237:$[+0.04,+0.13]$). That is the same pattern the final-quality analysis found
1238:on this solver: every single-generator pool harms it and the mixed pool
1239:does not. On fEABCLS the three competitive pools --- V2H, V2 and MIX ---
1240:are statistically indistinguishable from the control ($p=1.00$ for V2 and
1241:MIX), while the three weak ones are significantly slower. Seeding buys that
1242:solver nothing in time.
1243:
1244:Mean and sign agree almost everywhere. They part on three arms: MIX on the
1245:GA, V2H on fEABCLS and GT on TSN2, where $\Delta$RMTT reaches significance
1246:and the sign test does not. In each the difference is concentrated in a
1247:minority of instances rather than being a general shift, which is exactly
1248:what the sign test is there to detect.
1249:
1250:Ten arm--solver pairs are significantly \emph{slower} to the target than
1251:their control, and MOR is the extreme of every one: it is slower on all
1252:four solvers and reaches the target on no instance at all on the GA and
1253:fEABCLS, where 59 of 61 comparisons go against it. GT is slower on two
1254:solvers and, revealingly, \emph{faster} on ABCE3 --- the solver that
1255:tolerates every pool. Both generators sit at the bottom of the quality
1256:ladder of Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}, so this is the one place in the
1257:study where seed quality and outcome move together.
1258:
1259:The contrast with a success-only tally is instructive. Computing medians
1260:over successes alone would have credited MOR on the GA with reaching the
1261:target at 71\% of budget, a figure derived from the 15\% of instances where
1262:it succeeds at all.
1263:
1264:\subsection{Composition and fraction are two mechanisms, not
1265:one}\label{sec:res-fraction}
1266:
1267:The design carries two claims about why seeding works, and until now they
1268:were measured on different pools: \emph{composition}, from the mixed pool
1269:against the single-generator ones at full strength, and \emph{fraction},
1270:from the half-seeded learned arm against the fully seeded one. Whether they
1271:are the same mechanism seen twice or two that add is answered by the arm
1272:that combines them.
1273:
1274:On the genetic algorithm they add. Neither half-seeding the learned pool
1275:($+0.14$ points of RPD, $p=0.36$) nor mixing at full strength ($+0.06$,
1276:$p=0.55$) improves this solver. Doing both does: MIXH reaches $-0.28$
1277:points, interval $[-0.47,-0.10]$, $p=0.022$, and beats each of its
1278:components directly --- $-0.33$ against MIX and $-0.42$ against V2H, both
1279:$p<10^{-3}$ on paired per-instance differences. It is the only arm in the
1280:study that improves the genetic algorithm at its budget.
1281:
1282:The time-to-target evidence points the same way and is stronger, since it
1283:survives correction. Against its control the mixed pool at full strength is
1284:\emph{later} on this solver (18 earlier, 37 later); at half strength the
1285:sign inverts to 35 earlier and 19 later, $p=0.013$, with restricted mean
1286:time falling from $0.70$ to $0.62$ --- the control's own value. Half
1287:seeding does not merely reduce the delay that full seeding causes here; it
1288:removes it.
1289:
1290:Elsewhere the two mechanisms do not add, and on one solver they trade off.
1291:MIXH is the best arm on fEABCLS ($3.58\%$ against the control's $3.67\%$)
1292:and on TSN2 ($3.36\%$ against $3.60\%$), and on both it converts a
1293:time-to-target result that failed correction into one that passes: fEABCLS
1294:moves from 28/26 at $p=1.00$ to 37/17 at $p=0.010$, and TSN2 from 42/15 to
1295:46/13. But on ABCE3, the solver that consolidates seeds most readily, the
1296:full mixture remains better ($9.37\%$ against $9.43\%$; $+0.07$ in favour of
1297:MIX, $p=0.096$), and it is the one solver where retaining random material
1298:buys nothing.
1299:
1300:That pattern is the same one the convergence analysis produces. Retaining
1301:random material helps exactly where the solver cannot consolidate a fully
1302:seeded population inside its budget, and the ordering of solvers by how
1303:much it helps is the ordering by how badly they consolidate. We report this
1304:as a coherent association across three independent measurements ---
1305:converged quality, time to target, and the fraction contrast --- and not as
1306:an isolated effect. Two cautions bound it. The genetic algorithm's
1307:final-quality result does not survive correction over all 28 contrasts of
1308:the study, although its time-to-target result does; and only two fractions
1309:were run, so the shape of the interaction between them is unmeasured.
1310:
1311:\subsection{Initial-population quality: the mixed pool does not start
1312:better}\label{sec:res-seedqual}
1313:
1314:The obvious explanation for MIX's advantage would be that its seeds are
1315:simply better. They are not. Table~\ref{tab:seedqual} reports the quality
1316:of the initial population each arm actually receives, read from the
1317:step-zero record of the traces, so it is the solvers' own evaluation rather
1318:than the value stored with the pool.
1319:
1320:\begin{table}[htbp]
1321:\centering
1322:\caption{Quality of the seeded initial population relative to the unseeded
1323:control, averaged over five instances spanning $20\times20$ to
1324:$50\times20$ (TSN2 traces). Negative is better. ``Mean'' is over all 250
1325:initial individuals and is the quantity that characterises the pool.}
1326:\label{tab:seedqual}
1327:\begin{tabular}{lcc}
1328:\toprule
1329:Arm & Best individual & Mean of the 250 \\
1330:\midrule
1331:V2H & $-11.34\%$ & $-7.35\%$ \\
1332:V2  & $-11.84\%$ & $-14.73\%$ \\
1333:MOR & $-5.28\%$  & $-10.40\%$ \\
1334:GT  & $-4.75\%$  & $-8.85\%$ \\
1335:GP  & $\mathbf{-12.09\%}$ & $\mathbf{-15.48\%}$ \\
1336:MIX & $-12.25\%$ & $-13.12\%$ \\
1337:\bottomrule
1338:\end{tabular}
1339:\end{table}
1340:
1341:On the mean of the initial population --- the natural measure of pool
1342:quality --- MIX is \emph{worse} than both V2 and GP, by 1.6 and 2.4
1343:percentage points respectively, and it never holds the best individual by a
1344:meaningful margin. It nonetheless finishes ahead of both
1345:(Table~\ref{tab:rpd}). A pool that starts worse and ends better is direct
1346:evidence that the operative variable is composition, not seed quality.
1347:
1348:This also bears on a possible confound. The mixed pool introduces no new
1349:material: it is drawn from the same generator pools that feed the V2, GT
1350:and GP arms, taking the first $342/341/341$ entries of each. For GT and GP,
1351:whose pools are i.i.d., that is a sample of the same distribution the
1352:single-generator arm draws from. For v2 it is not: those first 342 entries
1353:are one training checkpoint, while the V2 arm cycles through all three. So
1354:no difference in \emph{provenance} explains the mixed pool's advantage, but
1355:its v2 component is narrower than the V2 arm's, not merely a re-mixture of
1356:it.
1357:
1358:\subsection{Which seeding strategy? Generator ranking and its
1359:solver-dependence}\label{sec:res-ranking}
1360:
1361:The practitioner's question is not whether seeding helps in general but
1362:which pool to use, and whether that choice must be re-made for every
1363:solver. We answer both with a Friedman test over the eight arms with the 61
1364:instances as blocks, followed by Holm-corrected paired post-hoc
1365:comparisons against the top-ranked arm of each solver.
1366:Table~\ref{tab:ranking} reports the mean ranks.
1367:
1368:\begin{table}[htbp]
1369:\centering
1370:\caption{Mean Friedman ranks of the eight arms (1 = best) over 61
1371:instances. Friedman rejects rank equality for every solver
1372:($p<10^{-15}$ throughout). Boldface marks arms not significantly worse
1373:than that solver's top-ranked arm (Holm-corrected paired Wilcoxon,
1374:$\alpha=0.05$).}
1375:\label{tab:ranking}
1376:\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}
1377:\toprule
1378:Solver & A0 & V2H & V2 & MOR & GT & GP & MIX \\
1379:\midrule
1380:GA      & \textbf{2.69} & \textbf{3.08} & 4.25 & 6.41 & 4.77 & 3.85 & \textbf{2.95} \\
1381:ABCE3   & 5.60 & \textbf{3.08} & \textbf{2.50} & 6.14 & 4.96 & 3.31 & \textbf{2.41} \\
1382:fEABCLS & \textbf{2.99} & \textbf{2.93} & \textbf{3.04} & 6.52 & 5.51 & 4.24 & \textbf{2.78} \\
1383:TSN2    & 3.40 & 3.93 & 3.80 & 5.37 & 5.07 & 3.85 & \textbf{2.57} \\
1384:\midrule
1385:\bottomrule
1386:\end{tabular}
1387:\end{table}
1388:
1389:Two findings follow, and they answer different questions.
1390:
1391:\paragraph{Which generator: the answer varies little across solvers.}
1392:The ranking of the seven seeded arms is highly consistent across the
1393:portfolio: restricted to those arms, the mean pairwise Spearman correlation
1394:between the four rank vectors is $\rho=0.88$. MIX is the best
1395:\emph{seeded} arm for all four solvers, with ranks between 2.41 and 2.95,
1396:and is never significantly worse than the top-ranked arm of any solver ---
1397:indeed it is the top-ranked arm outright for three of the four. MOR is last
1398:for all four (5.37 to 6.52) and GT second-to-last for three. With six arms
1399:and four solvers a rank correlation of $0.88$ is a descriptive summary
1400:rather than a demonstration of invariance, and the intermediate positions
1401:do move: GP outranks V2 on the GA but not elsewhere. What is stable is the
1402:top and the bottom, which is what a practitioner needs: within this
1403:portfolio, use a mixed pool and avoid the low-diversity constructive pools,
1404:without re-tuning that choice per algorithm.
1405:
1406:\paragraph{Whether to seed at all: the answer does depend on the solver.}
1407:The only arm whose rank moves substantially across solvers is the unseeded
1408:control, from 2.69 on the GA (where it is the top-ranked arm overall) to
1409:5.60 on ABCE3 (where it ranks sixth of seven, beaten by every seeded arm
1410:except MOR). Including A0 drags the mean pairwise agreement down from
1411:$\rho=0.88$ to $\rho=0.69$, and the weakest pair, GA versus ABCE3, rises
1412:from $\rho=0.36$ to $\rho=0.83$ once the control is removed. The
1413:displacement of A0 is thus the entire source of cross-solver disagreement.
1414:
1415:This dissociation is the cleanest statement of the paper's result. The
1416:question ``which seeds are good?'' has a solver-independent answer, because
1417:it is a property of the pool. The question ``should I seed?'' does not,
1418:because it depends on whether the solver's variation operators can exploit
1419:the pool --- the mechanism analysed in Section~\ref{sec:whyga}. Reporting
1420:only the first, as seeding studies commonly do, produces a recommendation
1421:that silently fails on solvers like the GA.
1422:
1423:\paragraph{Why the advantage is not an artefact of sampling.} A natural
1424:objection is that MIX might win because it exposes the search to more
1425:distinct seed material. Three properties of the design rule this out. The
1426:five pools are the same size ($L=1024$), so every arm draws 30 blocks of
1427:250 under the same arithmetic. The mixed pool is assembled from precisely
1428:the solutions that feed the V2, GT and GP arms, so it contains no material
1429:those arms do not also see. And directly measured
1430:(Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}), the population MIX actually receives is
1431:on average \emph{worse} than the ones V2 and GP receive, yet it converges
1432:to better solutions. Sampling breadth and seed quality both point the wrong
1433:way for the objection; within-population generator diversity is what
1434:remains.
1435:
1436:\subsection{The pilot's five statements, at scale}\label{sec:res-hyp}
