1482:
1483:\subsection{Threats addressed by design}\label{sec:res-threats}
1484:
1485:Before the checks themselves, one inferential property of the time-to-target
1486:design needs establishing rather than assuming. Its target is
1487:\emph{endogenous}: it is drawn from the control's own endpoint, so the
1488:control is measured against a quantity taken from its own realisation while
1489:the seeded arm is not. That asymmetry is not obviously harmless, and it is
1490:not enough to observe that censoring cannot reverse a sign. We therefore
1491:measured it. Splitting each instance's thirty control runs at random into
1492:two halves of fifteen, averaging each half and treating one as control and
1493:the other as arm, reproduces the analysis under a null that is true by
1494:construction. Over 400 such replicates the sign test rejects at $4.3\%$ on
1495:the GA, $6.8\%$ on ABCE3, $4.0\%$ on fEABCLS and $3.3\%$ on TSN2 against a
1496:nominal $5\%$, so the procedure is close to calibrated. It is not exactly
1497:so: under the null the seeded arm counts as earlier on only $46.2\%$,
1498:$45.3\%$, $47.3\%$ and $48.7\%$ of instances respectively, a small tilt
1499:against seeding. Every sign test in Table~\ref{tab:ttt} is taken against
1500:that solver's own null proportion rather than against $0.5$, which is what
1501:removes the tilt; the effect is visible on the GA, where the mixed pool
1502:reads as significantly slower against $0.5$ and does not against $0.462$.
1503:
1504:Two properties of the plateau rule of Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} qualify
1505:what it can be asked to support. Its \emph{counts} are sensitive to how it
1506:is set:
1507:converged cells move from 114 at a $0.05\%$ threshold to 162 at $0.25\%$,
1508:and from 194 to 63 as the fraction of runs required to satisfy it goes from
1509:half to all. Only the conclusions drawn from those counts are reported as
1510:stable, and only where they are. And on TS-N2, 325 of the 1830 control runs
1511:record a \emph{negative} improvement, down to $-1.95\%$: the framework's
1512:statistic takes the best individual under the solver's own ranking, which
1513:for TS-N2 is LEX2, so the recorded incumbent's midpoint need not be
1514:monotone even though its LEX2 value is. Those runs satisfy a rule that asks
1515:for improvement below $0.1\%$ for a reason unrelated to convergence, which
1516:inflates that solver's converged count by an amount we cannot separate
1517:cleanly.
1518:
1519:Three further checks guard the numbers above. First, expected makespans were
1520:recomputed by an \emph{independent} verifier: a standalone program that
1521:shares no code with the solver. It re-implements the instance parser, the
1522:interval arithmetic and the decoding from scratch, depends only on the
1523:standard library, and includes no header of the framework, so a defect in
1524:the solver's scheduling or interval classes cannot hide behind it. The
1525:sample is systematic rather than random --- every combination of the four
1526:solvers and seven arms on one instance from each of the seven size classes,
1527:980 stored solutions in total --- so each solver, arm and class is
1528:represented.
1529:
1530:The verifier reproduces the reported interval makespan exactly in 979 of
1531:the 980 cases. This is the expected outcome once one accounts for
1532:Lamarckian replacement: after local search the framework re-encodes the
1533:improved schedule into the chromosome, so the stored sequence already
1534:carries the machine orders of the schedule that was actually executed, and
1535:decoding it returns that same schedule. The single exception, one run on
1536:\texttt{tai50\_20\_09}, differs by 0.5 units of $\mathbb{E}[C_{\max}]$ on a
1537:value of 3119 --- $0.016\%$, and in the direction that makes the reported
1538:figure the more pessimistic. We have not traced its cause and record it as
1539:an open point. Second, every arm of a solver runs on the same instance
1540:with
1541:the same seed indices and the same budget, so the design is blocked by
1542:instance and no arm is systematically advantaged; the qualification of
1543:Section~\ref{sec:design} on what that pairing does and does not guarantee
1544:applies. Third, the stopping rule is a fixed CPU-time budget per size
1545:class, identical across arms, which eliminates the stagnation-triggered
1546:artefact documented in Section~\ref{sec:pilot}.
1547:
1548:\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
1549:
1550:\subsection{Seeding quality is not the operative variable}
1551:
1552:The pre-specified hypothesis --- that seeding helps precisely those solvers
1553:whose own search saturates below the seed level --- is not supported. The
1554:ordering by unseeded quality and the ordering by seeding effect do not
1555:match: the weakest solver gains most, the strongest gains a little, a
1556:mid-ranked one is unaffected and another is harmed at the budget and helped
1557:once it suffices. Solver quality alone does not predict the sign.
1558:
1559:Two variables do carry information, and the design supports them as
1560:associations rather than isolating them as causes. The first is the
1561:\emph{budget relative to what the solver needs}. Section~\ref{sec:res-conv}
1562:shows the GA reversal to be a budget effect, and Section~\ref{sec:res-size}
1563:shows the TSN2 gain confined to the class where the budget is tightest
1564:relative to instance size. The two findings point the same way: seeding
1565:matters most where the solver has least time to build structure itself, and
1566:its apparent sign can flip when that time is short enough.
1567:
1568:The second is pool composition. MIX --- equal thirds of v2, GT and GP,
1569:i.e.\ 84/83/83 of the 250 seeded individuals --- holds the best Friedman
1570:rank among seeded arms on all four solvers, while the single-generator
1571:pools of comparable individual quality (V2, GP) are weaker and the
1572:low-diversity pools (MOR, GT) are actively harmful. Since MIX is assembled
1573:from exactly the solutions that feed V2, GT and GP, and starts from a worse
1574:average population than either V2 or GP
1575:(Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}), neither provenance nor seed quality can
1576:account for its advantage. What we cannot do is separate diversity from
1577:the other properties a mixture has --- coverage of distinct basins,
1578:complementarity between specific pairs of generators --- because we did not
1579:run pools matched on quality with diversity varied independently. The
1580:composition claim is therefore an association supported by an elimination
1581:of the obvious alternatives, not a controlled result.
1582:
1583:\subsection{Why the genetic algorithm is harmed: a failure to
1584:consolidate}\label{sec:whyga}
1585:
1586:The intuitive explanation for a seeding-induced loss is premature
1587:convergence: a population of good, similar individuals collapses onto a
1588:local optimum. The data do not support it. We instrumented the GA and
1589:ABCE3 with the mean pairwise Hamming distance of the population, sampled
1590:every 10\,s over 10 runs on four medium instances (normalised to $[0,1]$,
1591:where 0 denotes an identical population). Table~\ref{tab:hamming} reports
1592:the result. The seeded GA is not less diverse than its control --- it is
1593:\emph{significantly more} diverse, by $+24.3\%$ under V2 and $+13.7\%$
1594:under MIX (paired Wilcoxon, $p<10^{-4}$), and it ends the run at $0.720$
1595:against the control's $0.464$.
1596:
1597:\begin{table}[htbp]
1598:\centering
1599:\caption{Mean pairwise Hamming distance of the population (0 = identical
1600:population, 1 = maximal positional difference), averaged over four
1601:instances and 10 runs. The seeded GA converges structurally \emph{less}
1602:than its control; ABCE3 is unaffected by seeding.}
1603:\label{tab:hamming}
1604:\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
1605:\toprule
1606:Solver & Arm & Start & Mean & End & Drop \\
1607:\midrule
1608:\multirow{3}{*}{GA}
1609: & A0  & 0.9474 & 0.7646 & 0.4639 & $-51.0\%$ \\
1610: & V2  & 0.9310 & 0.8845 & 0.7195 & $-22.7\%$ \\
1611: & MIX & 0.9301 & 0.8350 & 0.5552 & $-40.3\%$ \\
1612:\midrule
1613:\multirow{3}{*}{ABCE3}
1614: & A0  & 0.9474 & 0.7140 & 0.8541 & $-9.9\%$ \\
1615: & V2  & 0.9310 & 0.7059 & 0.8602 & $-7.6\%$ \\
1616: & MIX & 0.9301 & 0.7171 & 0.8497 & $-8.6\%$ \\
1617:\bottomrule
1618:\end{tabular}
1619:\end{table}
1620:
1621:The failure is therefore the opposite of premature convergence: within a
1622:fixed budget the seeded GA never consolidates. Its control contracts by
1623:$51.0\%$ in structural spread and exploits the region it has found; under
1624:V2 the seeded variant contracts by only $22.7\%$ and is still exploring
1625:when the budget expires. The proximate cause is that the seeds, being
1626:drawn from generators with different construction logic, are good but
1627:mutually \emph{dissimilar}; JOX recombining two structurally divergent
1628:parents produces offspring that inherit the operation ordering of neither,
1629:so selection cannot accumulate structure. The harm grows with instance size
1630:(Section~\ref{sec:res-size}) because longer chromosomes need more
1631:generations to consolidate, and the budget does not grow proportionally.
1632:
1633:The contrast with ABCE3 is consistent with this reading. Seeding does not
1634:perturb ABCE3's diversity trajectory at all (differences of $-2.3\%$ and
1635:$+4.0\%$, $p=0.27$ and $p=0.90$), and its population stays dispersed
1636:throughout ($0.85$ at the end). ABCE3 does not rely on recombining
1637:population members to build structure, so seeds are retained and refined
1638:rather than destroyed.
1639:
1640:The ordering of the two seeded arms is itself informative. V2, drawn from a
1641:single generator, produces the least consolidation ($-22.7\%$) and the
1642:largest loss in final quality; MIX, the more heterogeneous pool,
1643:consolidates further ($-40.3\%$) and is the better of the two arms on this
1644:solver. Structural heterogeneity of the \emph{pool} and of the
1645:\emph{surviving population} are therefore not the same quantity: what slows
1646:the GA is a population its operator cannot consolidate, not a diverse pool
1647:as such.
1648:
1649:Three limits on this account should be stated plainly. First, it is
1650:consistent with the convergence analysis rather than independent of it:
1651:Section~\ref{sec:res-conv} shows the GA's loss disappears where its control
1652:converges, which is what a consolidation-rate explanation predicts, but
1653:both observations rest on the same runs. Second, GA and ABCE3 differ in
1654:selection, replacement, mutation and population dynamics as well as in
1655:recombination, so attributing the contrast to JOX specifically is
1656:under-determined; establishing it would require holding the rest of the GA
1657:fixed and varying the crossover, which we did not do. Third, mean pairwise
1658:Hamming distance is a genotypic measure on a representation with repeated
1659:symbols, and need not track the building blocks JOX actually manipulates;
1660:a measure of parent--offspring precedence preservation would test the
1661:mechanism more directly. We therefore offer consolidation failure as the
1662:reading the present data favour, not as a demonstrated cause.
1663:
1664:\subsection{Practitioner guidance}
1665:
1666:The results support four recommendations, each with its scope attached.
1667:(i) Seed with a \emph{mixed} pool, and default to replacing \emph{half} the
1668:population rather than all of it. The mixed pool outranks every
1669:single-generator pool on all four solvers, and drawing on three generators
1670:costs no more than drawing on one since the pool is the same size; the half
1671:strength variant is the best arm on three of the four, and the only one that
1672:helps the genetic algorithm. Seed the whole population only when the solver
1673:consolidates readily, as the elitist ABC does. What is not free is producing
1674:the pool: at the tenth of the budget where the advantage is largest,
1675:generating a run's seeds costs several times that slice unless the pool is
1676:reused across runs (Section~\ref{sec:gencost}). Seed once, solve many. (ii)
1677:Expect the benefit to grow as the budget tightens relative to instance
1678:size, and expect its \emph{sign} to be unreliable when the budget is far
1679:from sufficient --- that is where our genetic algorithm reverses. (iii)
1680:Before concluding that seeding fails on a recombination-based solver,
1681:check whether its control has converged; on our data that distinction
1682:separates a genuine null from an artefact of the budget. (iv) Do not assume
1683:a seeded fraction transfers between solvers. Seeding the whole population
1684:is better on the elitist ABC and worse on the genetic algorithm, both at
1685:$p<10^{-3}$, and neither is detectable without running the contrast: the
1686:half-seeded and fully seeded arms differ by at most $0.39$ points of RPD,
1687:well inside the spread that separates the generators themselves.
1688:
1689:\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:limits}
1690:
1691:\paragraph{Scope.} The study covers one problem family (interval JSP), one
1692:encoding (permutation with repetition), and four solvers three of which
1693:share the ABC framework or components of it. Nothing here licenses a
1694:general statement about recombinative metaheuristics or about seeding at
1695:large; the conclusions are about this problem, this representation and
1696:these operators. Replication on the deterministic JSP, on a different
1697:uncertainty model, or on an external benchmark would be the natural next
1698:test.
1699:
1700:\paragraph{Mechanism.} The two explanatory claims --- pool composition
1701:rather than seed quality, and consolidation failure rather than premature
1702:convergence --- are associations that survive the obvious alternatives, not
1703:controlled results. Section~\ref{sec:whyga} states the three specific
1704:limits on the second of them.
1705:
1706:\paragraph{Budgets and cost.} Budgets are CPU-time caps measured with the
1707:process clock on a single Linux machine at a fixed occupancy of fourteen
1708:concurrent workers. Within a cell every arm shares the cap, so the paired
1709:comparisons that carry every claim here are unaffected, but the absolute
1710:level is not portable: the work a CPU-second buys depends on contention for
1711:cache and memory bandwidth among the concurrent workers. Our absolute RPDs
1712:should not be compared against figures published for these solvers
1713:elsewhere, and more generally a single absolute quality figure reported
1714:under a time budget, without the hardware occupancy that produced it, is
1715:less reproducible than it appears. Section~\ref{sec:gencost} reports the
1716:cost of producing the pools, with two gaps: the training of the learned
1717:policy is excluded as amortised across instances, and only the two
1718:heuristic generators were timed directly, the learned policy's pool cost
1719:being inferred from per-rollout inference and MOR-$\varepsilon$'s not
1720:measured at all.
1721:
1722:\paragraph{The v2 pool is blocked, not i.i.d.} Its 1{,}024 entries are three
1723:consecutive blocks, one per training checkpoint, and the blocks differ in
1724:quality. Because an arm draws a contiguous window whose start advances with
1725:the run index, the checkpoint mixture varies from run to run: several V2
1726:runs and twenty of the thirty V2H runs are fed by a single checkpoint. Over
1727:thirty runs the mixture averages close to balanced ($91/80/80$ of 250 for
1728:V2), so the effect on the per-instance means that carry our analysis is
1729:small, but the between-run variance of the two v2 arms is inflated relative
1730:to every other arm, and inflated in a structured rather than random way. The
1731:mixed pool inherits the head of that pool, so its v2 third is one
1732:checkpoint. Interleaving the checkpoints, as the mixed pool interleaves its
1733:generators, would remove this.
1734:
1735:\paragraph{Design gaps.} The seeded fraction $p^{*}=100\%$ was selected on
1736:two solvers and eight instances, so it is a protocol decision for this
1737:study rather than a generally preferable fraction, and the half-seeded arm
1738:shows the fraction interacting with the solver in both directions
1739:(Section~\ref{sec:res-anytime}). Only two fractions were run, so the shape
1740:of that interaction between them is unmeasured.
1741:The mixed pool was not compared against two-generator mixtures or against
1742:alternative proportions, so we cannot attribute its advantage to any
1743:particular pairing. And the structural-diversity instrumentation covers
1744:four instances and two solvers, a narrow base for the argument it supports.
1745:
1746:\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}
1747:
1748:We conducted, to our knowledge, the largest controlled study of population
1749:seeding for the interval job shop: four solvers, eight initialisation arms,
1750:61 benchmark instances and 30 runs per cell --- 58{,}560 runs --- evaluated
1751:against published lower bounds under a fixed-budget protocol designed to
1752:eliminate three artefacts we document in a pilot study.
1753:
1754:Seeding is not uniformly beneficial, and its sign is not predicted by
1755:solver strength. Analysed with the instance as the unit, a mixed pool
1756:improves the weakest solver decisively ($-1.14$ points of RPD, 95\% CI
1757:$[-1.36,-0.93]$), leaves a memetic ABC unchanged, and does not harm a
1758:genetic algorithm, though every single-generator pool does. On the
1759:strongest solver the mean improvement is small, concentrated in one size
1760:class, short of significance under an instance-weighted test, and absent
1761:altogether on the instances where that solver's own control converges; we
1762:report it as a bounded result rather than as an improvement of the state
1763:of the art.
1764:
1765:Three findings seem to us to travel beyond this problem family. First,
1766:\emph{the budget decides the sign, not just the size}. The two effects at
1767:the ends of our portfolio both move when the analysis is restricted to
1768:cells whose control actually converged, and they move in opposite
1769:directions: the genetic algorithm's apparent damage disappears, and the
1770:tabu hybrid's gain disappears with it. Its benefit is confined to the
1771:largest instances, which are exactly the cells where its budget did not
1772:suffice to converge. Neither reversal is visible in a table that pools
1773:converged and budget-limited cells. A seeding study that fixes one budget
1774:without checking convergence can therefore report either sign, and which
1775:one it reports is not predictable from the solver's strength.
1776:
1777:Second, \emph{what a pool is made of, and how much of the population it
1778:replaces, are two separable levers}. The mixed pool draws on the solutions
1779:that feed the single-generator arms and receives a worse average initial
1780:population than two of them, yet outranks all of them: composition, not
1781:seed quality, orders the pools that help. Independently of that, the
1782:fraction seeded interacts with the solver, and in both directions ---
1783:seeding half the population is better on the genetic algorithm and worse on
1784:the elitist ABC. The two levers add rather than substitute: neither alone
1785:improves the genetic algorithm and their combination does, and that
1786:combination is the best arm on three of the four solvers. Seed quality is
1787:not irrelevant at the bottom of the ladder --- MOR, the weakest generator,
1788:is significantly slower to the control's own final quality on all four
1789:solvers --- but among the pools that help it is not what separates them.
1790:
1791:Third, \emph{the failure mode is not the one usually assumed}. Premature
1792:convergence is the standard explanation for seeding going wrong; our
1793:diversity measurements show the opposite, a population that stays dispersed
1794:and never consolidates within the budget. We advance consolidation failure
1795:as the reading the data favour, while noting that separating it from the
1796:other differences between the two solvers would need ablations this design
1797:does not contain.
1798:
1799:The practical consequence is that seeding should be treated as an
1800:interaction between pool composition, variation operator and budget,
1801:evaluated at the instance size of the intended application, rather than as
1802:a property of the seeds alone. And the methodological consequence is that
1803:the three habits our pilot identified --- stagnation-triggered stopping,
1804:single-budget comparison, and self-referential speed metrics --- are enough
1805:to manufacture a positive seeding result where none exists, or to hide one
1806:that is there.
