Metric A behaves exactly as the pilot warned, and one figure shows why. On
the GA the earliest plateau belongs to MOR ($0.49$ of the budget against the
control's $0.60$), and MOR is the worst generator on all four solvers: an
arm that settles quickly on a poor value scores well \emph{because} it is
poor. We report the metric to document that it must not be used alone, and
do not tabulate it further.

Metric B needs care of a different kind. An arm that never reaches the
target on an instance has no time-to-target there, and dropping such
instances would summarise only the successes --- precisely discarding the
cases where the arm does worst. The reach rate and the times must therefore
enter the analysis together.

Two properties of the design constrain how. First, the arms are paired:
each seeded arm and its control share the instance, the budget and the
target, so a comparison that treats them as independent groups is not

exec
"C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command 'Get-Content final/paper/main.tex | Select-Object -Skip 1200 -First 400' in C:\Users\diazhernan\CLionProjects\RL Seeds\IJSP
 succeeded in 542ms:
available. Second, and more restrictive, the target on an instance is the
control's own endpoint, so the control attains it on all 61 instances by
construction. Its sample cannot be censored, whereas the seeded samples
can. A log-rank test against such a reference contrasts a censored sample
with one that is uncensorable by definition, and we therefore do not report
one.

What survives both constraints is a paired analysis of the recorded times,
with a non-reacher recorded at the budget, $T=1.0$. The quantity the mean
then estimates is the restricted mean time $E[\min(T,1)]$, for which that
recording is a definition rather than an approximation. The primary
contrast is the sign test on the paired earlier/later counts, ties
excluded, taken against the null proportion the design itself produces
rather than against $0.5$: because the target is drawn from the control's
own endpoint, a split-half calibration under a true null
(Section~\ref{sec:res-threats}) puts that proportion at $0.46$--$0.49$
depending on the solver. The paired difference in restricted mean time
($\Delta$RMTT) is reported alongside it with a bootstrap interval over
instances. Both are Holm-corrected over the seven seeded arms of each solver.
No test is reported for the control, which attains its own target on all 61
instances by construction; ``reach'' is the percentage of instances on
which an arm attains it at all.

Under this treatment ABCE3 stands out. Its mixed pool reaches the control's
\emph{final} quality on every instance and earlier on 57 of the 59 that are
not ties, cutting restricted mean time from $0.38$ to $0.12$ of the budget;
V2 and V2H do nearly as well. These are unambiguous ``same quality,
sooner'' results, and they agree with the converged-quality finding for
that solver. TSN2 joins it more modestly: its mixed pool is earlier on 42
instances and later on 15 ($p=0.003$), and reaches the target on $93\%$ of
them against the control's $100\%$ by construction.

The other two solvers give the opposite answer. \emph{Five of the GA's six
seeded arms are significantly slower} to the control's own final quality;
only the mixed pool escapes, and narrowly (18 earlier, 37 later,
$p=0.057$), though its restricted mean time is clearly worse ($+0.09$,
$[+0.04,+0.13]$). That is the same pattern the final-quality analysis found
on this solver: every single-generator pool harms it and the mixed pool
does not. On fEABCLS the three competitive pools --- V2H, V2 and MIX ---
are statistically indistinguishable from the control ($p=1.00$ for V2 and
MIX), while the three weak ones are significantly slower. Seeding buys that
solver nothing in time.

Mean and sign agree almost everywhere. They part on three arms: MIX on the
GA, V2H on fEABCLS and GT on TSN2, where $\Delta$RMTT reaches significance
and the sign test does not. In each the difference is concentrated in a
minority of instances rather than being a general shift, which is exactly
what the sign test is there to detect.

Ten arm--solver pairs are significantly \emph{slower} to the target than
their control, and MOR is the extreme of every one: it is slower on all
four solvers and reaches the target on no instance at all on the GA and
fEABCLS, where 59 of 61 comparisons go against it. GT is slower on two
solvers and, revealingly, \emph{faster} on ABCE3 --- the solver that
tolerates every pool. Both generators sit at the bottom of the quality
ladder of Section~\ref{sec:res-seedqual}, so this is the one place in the
study where seed quality and outcome move together.

The contrast with a success-only tally is instructive. Computing medians
over successes alone would have credited MOR on the GA with reaching the
target at 71\% of budget, a figure derived from the 15\% of instances where
it succeeds at all.

\subsection{Composition and fraction are two mechanisms, not
one}\label{sec:res-fraction}

The design carries two claims about why seeding works, and until now they
were measured on different pools: \emph{composition}, from the mixed pool
against the single-generator ones at full strength, and \emph{fraction},
from the half-seeded learned arm against the fully seeded one. Whether they
are the same mechanism seen twice or two that add is answered by the arm
that combines them.

On the genetic algorithm they add. Neither half-seeding the learned pool
($+0.14$ points of RPD, $p=0.36$) nor mixing at full strength ($+0.06$,
$p=0.55$) improves this solver. Doing both does: MIXH reaches $-0.28$
points, interval $[-0.47,-0.10]$, $p=0.022$, and beats each of its
components directly --- $-0.33$ against MIX and $-0.42$ against V2H, both
$p<10^{-3}$ on paired per-instance differences. It is the only arm in the
study that improves the genetic algorithm at its budget.

The time-to-target evidence points the same way and is stronger, since it
survives correction. Against its control the mixed pool at full strength is
\emph{later} on this solver (18 earlier, 37 later); at half strength the
sign inverts to 35 earlier and 19 later, $p=0.013$, with restricted mean
time falling from $0.70$ to $0.62$ --- the control's own value. Half
seeding does not merely reduce the delay that full seeding causes here; it
removes it.

Elsewhere the two mechanisms do not add, and on one solver they trade off.
MIXH is the best arm on fEABCLS ($3.58\%$ against the control's $3.67\%$)
and on TSN2 ($3.36\%$ against $3.60\%$), and on both it converts a
time-to-target result that failed correction into one that passes: fEABCLS
moves from 28/26 at $p=1.00$ to 37/17 at $p=0.010$, and TSN2 from 42/15 to
46/13. But on ABCE3, the solver that consolidates seeds most readily, the
full mixture remains better ($9.37\%$ against $9.43\%$; $+0.07$ in favour of
MIX, $p=0.096$), and it is the one solver where retaining random material
buys nothing.

That pattern is the same one the convergence analysis produces. Retaining
random material helps exactly where the solver cannot consolidate a fully
seeded population inside its budget, and the ordering of solvers by how
much it helps is the ordering by how badly they consolidate. We report this
as a coherent association across three independent measurements ---
converged quality, time to target, and the fraction contrast --- and not as
an isolated effect. Two cautions bound it. The genetic algorithm's
final-quality result does not survive correction over all 28 contrasts of
the study, although its time-to-target result does; and only two fractions
were run, so the shape of the interaction between them is unmeasured.

\subsection{Initial-population quality: the mixed pool does not start
better}\label{sec:res-seedqual}

The obvious explanation for MIX's advantage would be that its seeds are
simply better. They are not. Table~\ref{tab:seedqual} reports the quality
of the initial population each arm actually receives, read from the
step-zero record of the traces, so it is the solvers' own evaluation rather
than the value stored with the pool.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Quality of the seeded initial population relative to the unseeded
control, averaged over five instances spanning $20\times20$ to
$50\times20$ (TSN2 traces). Negative is better. ``Mean'' is over all 250
initial individuals and is the quantity that characterises the pool.}
\label{tab:seedqual}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Arm & Best individual & Mean of the 250 \\
\midrule
V2H & $-11.34\%$ & $-7.35\%$ \\
V2  & $-11.84\%$ & $-14.73\%$ \\
MOR & $-5.28\%$  & $-10.40\%$ \\
GT  & $-4.75\%$  & $-8.85\%$ \\
GP  & $\mathbf{-12.09\%}$ & $\mathbf{-15.48\%}$ \\
MIX & $-12.25\%$ & $-13.12\%$ \\
\bottomrule
